Check your Git configuration; something seems to be wrong[1].  By
default, 'git commit' will open vi or some such editor, already filled
in with some commented out stuff (instructions, a list of changed
files and so on).  Then you're supposed to write at least a one-liner,
save and exit the editor -- or Git will refuse to commit.

> Presumably there is another way to include longer messages, I'll
> look into it next time I do a more complicated commit. Now that you've
> told me how github handles the summary line [...]

Side note: I think it's also common for non-Github users to just see
the summary.  Try 'git log --oneline --decorate --all --graph' for
example -- it's the way I normally look at my history.

/Jorgen

[1] Unless you explicitly give a message as an argument to git commit,
    but it doesn't sound as if you do.

